---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-14",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-14",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "streamdale folding treadmill",
      "streamdale treadmill",
      "streamdale furniture treadmill",
      "streamdale treadmill review",
      "simplie fun treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See What to Check", link: "#what-to-check" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/reviews/">Reviews</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Model Review</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Streamdale Folding Treadmill: Who Sells It and What to Check</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Streamdale is not a treadmill brand with a model line. It is an online furniture seller whose catalogue includes a rotating set of unbranded folding treadmills, also listed on department-store marketplaces. That changes what a useful review looks like.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 14, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#the-listings" class="btn-ghost">The Listings</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          A search for the Streamdale folding treadmill suggests a single machine you could look up,
          compare and buy. What you actually find is a seller. Streamdale Furniture runs an online
          shop that sells sofas, ottomans and bed frames alongside a handful of folding treadmills,
          each listed by a long descriptive title rather than a model number, and the same listings
          appear on marketplace storefronts at department-store sites. This page explains who
          Streamdale is, what its treadmill listings say and do not say, what its return and
          shipping terms mean for a heavy machine, and how to decide whether one is worth buying.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          14 October 2026 we read Streamdale Furniture’s website, including its treadmill collection,
          refund, shipping and contact policies, and recorded the title, price, listed weight and
          claims for every treadmill shown. We compared those with Streamdale listings on two
          department-store marketplaces, the seller’s Better Business Bureau profile, and the CPSC
          recall database. We found no independent review of any Streamdale treadmill, so no
          specification on this page is confirmed: every figure quoted is the seller’s claim, and we
          give none as a fact about the machine. We have not tested any Streamdale treadmill. The
          catalogue changes, and prices and listings may differ by the time you read this.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Is the Streamdale Folding Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-is-streamdale">Who Is Streamdale Furniture?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-listings">The Streamdale Treadmill Listings, and How to Read Them</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reading-the-claims">Reading the Motor, Capacity and Belt Claims</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-returns-shipping">Streamdale Warranty, Returns and Shipping</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-check">What to Check Before You Buy a Streamdale Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-it-suits">Who a Streamdale Folding Treadmill Might Suit</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">Alternatives on This Site</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is the Streamdale Folding Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>There is no single Streamdale folding treadmill to judge: Streamdale Furniture is
            a seller, not a treadmill maker, and its folding treadmills are unbranded machines listed
            without model numbers, so none of their specifications can be checked against an
            independent source.</strong> We found no independent review of any of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            That does not make every Streamdale treadmill a poor machine. Some of the listings
            describe ordinary budget folding treadmills — a 2.5 to 3.5 horsepower motor claim, a
            belt of roughly 39 to 43 inches, a top speed between 7.5 and 10 mph — at prices from
            about $360 to over $1,100. What it does mean is that you would be buying on the seller’s
            word alone, from a shop that lists no street address or phone number, under a return
            policy that excludes sale items and makes you pay to send a remorse return back.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our view is that at these prices you can buy a folding treadmill from a maker you can
            name, whose figures agree across sources and whose warranty is written down. If you are
            still drawn to a Streamdale listing, the checks in the <a href="#what-to-check" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what to check</a> section will tell you whether that particular one is worth the risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-streamdale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Is Streamdale Furniture?</h2>
<p>
            Streamdale Furniture describes itself as an online store for "everything home, office,
            and outdoors", and says its products come from "manufacturers all around the world". Its
            homepage also says it supplies furniture "directly to <a href="/home-depot-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Home Depot</a>, Wayfair, Lowe’s, and
            all other marketplaces without any markups".
          </p>
          <p>
            A few things about the business are worth knowing before you buy something heavy and
            powered from it:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The shop and Simplie Fun are linked.</strong> Streamdale’s website runs on an
              online-store account registered as Simplie Fun, and several of its treadmill listings
              name Simplie Fun as the vendor. At least one department-store marketplace listing
              carries both names.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact is by email, web form or chat.</strong> Streamdale’s published contact
              information gives an email address and no street address or phone number.</li>
            <li><strong>Its BBB profile is thin.</strong> The Better Business Bureau lists Streamdale
              Furniture in Lafayette, Colorado, as not accredited, with a rating in the B range and a
              handful of complaints over three years when we checked. BBB ratings change and are not
              a verdict on any product, but they are one of the few independent records of the
              business.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this is unusual for an online furniture reseller, and none of it is evidence of
            wrongdoing. It is relevant because a treadmill, unlike a sofa, has a motor and controller
            that can fail, and the seller is the only party you can identify if one does.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is Streamdale on Wayfair?</h3>
          <p>
            Streamdale says it supplies Wayfair, but when we searched Wayfair on 14 October 2026 we
            found no treadmill sold under the Streamdale name. That is not surprising. Wayfair sells
            much of what it gets from suppliers under its own house labels, so a supplier’s product
            would not necessarily carry the supplier’s name there. Our <a href="/wayfair-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to buying a treadmill on Wayfair</a> explains how that works, and our check of the <a href="/east-urban-home-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">East Urban Home compact foldable electric treadmill</a> shows what can go wrong with house-label treadmill listings.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where Streamdale treadmills are sold</h3>
          <p>
            We found Streamdale treadmill listings on its own website and on marketplace storefronts
            at Macy’s and Shop Simon; the Shop Simon listing we opened states that the item ships
            from and is sold by Streamdale Furniture rather than by the mall operator. On a marketplace listing like
            that, the department store hosts the page but the seller’s terms govern the sale, so
            check which policy applies before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Streamdale Treadmill Listings, and How to Read Them</h2>
<p>
            On 14 October 2026 Streamdale’s treadmill collection showed nine products: eight for
            people, of which six are full folding treadmills and two are walking pads, plus one pet
            treadmill. The table summarises the six folding treadmills as listed. Every figure is
            the seller’s claim.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listing (shortened)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price shown</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Seller’s main claims</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.5HP quiet folding treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$362.59</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.5 HP, to 7.5 mph, 5% incline, 300 lb, about 58 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Foldable 2.5HP electric treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$479.55</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.5 HP, 39.4 x 15.4 in. belt, 265 lb, about 57 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline treadmill, 3.0 HP, auto-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$493.62</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"Max" 3.0 HP, 10 mph, 350 lb, about 60 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Home foldable treadmill with incline, 5 in. LCD</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$493.62</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Manual incline, 250 lb, about 73 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding electric 3.5HP with incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$851.01 (sold out)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.5 HP "impulse", 0-9 mph, three manual incline levels, 330 lb, about 90 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Electric treadmill with automatic incline, 3.5HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,164.31</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.5 HP, 10 mph, 330 lb, about 132 lb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No model numbers, only titles and stock codes</h3>
          <p>
            None of the listings gives a manufacturer’s model number. Each has a long keyword title
            and a stock code of the kind used by wholesale catalogues. Without a model number you
            cannot search for the same machine elsewhere, find its manual, or check it against a
            recall notice, which is why we cannot verify any of these figures.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Signs the listings come from a supplier feed</h3>
          <p>
            Several specification blocks contained unfilled template fields — text such as
            "#originPlace#" and "#dangerFlag#" where a value should be — and some gave running-belt
            lengths with a foot mark where inches were plainly meant. Others listed the place of
            origin as China. That is consistent with Streamdale’s own description of sourcing from
            manufacturers worldwide and passing the products on to marketplaces. It also means the
            descriptions were written for a catalogue rather than checked against the machine in the
            box.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Prices that look like discounts</h3>
          <p>
            Prices on the seller’s own site ran from about $360 to about $1,160, with odd-cent
            figures typical of automated pricing. On one marketplace a Streamdale walking pad was
            shown at a sale price described as 41% off a higher regular price. Treat a regular price
            on a listing like this as a reference point, not a measure of value, and compare the
            sale price with what identifiable machines cost. Our <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cost guide</a> gives typical bands by tier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-the-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Reading the Motor, Capacity and Belt Claims</h2>
<p>
            Even without independent figures, the claims on the listings can be read for what they
            are likely to mean, using what is known about budget folding treadmills generally.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Horsepower without "continuous" is a peak figure</h3>
          <p>
            The listings quote 2.5, 3.0 and 3.5 HP, with words such as "max" and "impulse" attached
            to some. None says continuous. A peak rating describes a brief maximum, not what the
            motor sustains through a 40-minute session, and it cannot be compared with a continuous
            rating on another machine. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains the difference and why it matters most for heavier users and runners.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Capacity should be read against machine weight</h3>
          <p>
            One listing claims a 350 lb user limit on a machine it lists at about 60 lb. A weight
            capacity summarises the whole structure — frame, deck, rollers and motor — and a high
            figure on a light frame leaves little margin. That does not prove the figure wrong, but
            it is a reason to leave a generous gap between your weight and the claim. Our <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a> suggests buying a machine rated comfortably above your body weight.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Belt length decides whether you can run</h3>
          <p>
            Where the listings give a belt size, it is roughly 39 to 43 inches long and about 15 to
            16 inches wide. That is fine for walking and a light jog for many people, and short for
            a full running stride, particularly for taller users. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> works through stride length against belt length; most guidance puts a comfortable running
            belt well above these figures.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Noise figures are claims, not measurements</h3>
          <p>
            Ranges such as 40 to 55 dB appear on some listings with no indication of how, where or at
            what speed they were measured. On a folding treadmill footfall through the floor is often
            louder than the motor. Our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a> separates the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-returns-shipping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Streamdale Warranty, Returns and Shipping</h2>
<p>
            For a seller-only product these policies are the real specification, because they decide
            what happens if the machine arrives damaged, fails early or simply is not what you
            expected.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns: 30 days, unused, and not on sale items</h3>
          <p>
            Streamdale’s refund policy gives 30 days after you receive an item to request a return.
            The item must be unused, in its <a href="/how-to-pack-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>original packaging</a>, and you need proof of purchase,
            return tracking and photographs of the product and packaging. For a change-of-mind
            return you supply your own shipping label, and the policy says on-sale items are not
            returnable. Damaged or defective items are handled differently: you contact the seller
            with photos and a return label is provided.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two of those conditions bite hard on a treadmill. Unused is difficult to satisfy once you
            have assembled and walked on a machine to see whether it suits you. And if the price you
            paid was a sale price, the policy as written excludes the item from returns altogether.
            Ask the seller, in writing and before you order, whether the item you are buying can be
            returned and on what terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty: not clearly stated</h3>
          <p>
            Streamdale’s refund policy does not use the word warranty. The closest provision says you
            may request replacement parts free of charge. One marketplace listing for a Streamdale
            treadmill described replacement parts as available case by case within six months, which
            we saw in a single place and could not confirm. Neither is a motor warranty in the sense a
            treadmill maker would offer one. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains what cover is reasonable at this price.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shipping: ground parcel, to your door</h3>
          <p>
            The shipping policy gives a lead time of one to three days for in-stock items and two to
            seven days in transit, by FedEx Ground, FedEx Home or UPS Ground, free to the lower 48
            states and not to Alaska, Hawaii or US territories. Parcel delivery generally means the
            box is left at your door rather than carried to a room, and nothing in the policy mentions
            assembly or packaging removal. A boxed folding treadmill listed at 57 to 132 lb is a
            two-person lift. Our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a> and <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembly guide</a> cover what to plan for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Check Before You Buy a Streamdale Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            If a Streamdale listing still looks like the right machine at the right price, these
            checks will tell you whether that particular one is worth the risk.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Ask for the manufacturer and model number.</strong> Email the seller and ask
              who makes the machine and what its model number is. With it, you can search for a
              manual, other listings of the same machine and recall notices. Without it, you cannot.</li>
            <li><strong>Confirm the returns position for your exact order.</strong> Ask whether the
              listing counts as a sale item under the refund policy, whether an assembled machine can
              be returned, and roughly what return shipping on the boxed weight would cost.</li>
            <li><strong>Get warranty terms in writing.</strong> Ask how long the motor, controller and
              frame are covered, whether parts are supplied free, who pays shipping on parts, and how a
              claim is made.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the claims against each other.</strong> Make sure the title, bullet
              points and specification block agree on motor, speed, incline and capacity. If they
              disagree, ask which is right before ordering.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the reviews on the page you buy from.</strong> Reviews on a seller’s own
              site and on marketplaces may not describe the same machine. Read the low-star ones first
              and look for consistent problems. Our <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to reading treadmill reviews</a> covers what to watch for.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the space and the delivery.</strong> Measure the room, including
              clearance behind the deck, using our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>,
              and plan for a heavy box at the door.</li>
            <li><strong>Pay with protection.</strong> A credit card gives you a dispute route if the
              item never arrives or is not as described; keep every email with the seller.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            On 14 October 2026 we found no CPSC recall under the Streamdale or Simplie Fun names. A
            recall would name the manufacturer rather than the reseller, which is one more reason to
            find out who that is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who a Streamdale Folding Treadmill Might Suit</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A walker on a tight budget who has found a genuinely low price.</strong> If a
            listing at the bottom of the range meets your needs for walking and the occasional light
            jog, and you have confirmed returns and parts in writing, the risk may be proportionate
            to the outlay.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Someone who needs a specific feature the listing offers</strong> — a long
            handrail on the seniors’ walking pad, say — and can confirm it from the photographs and
            the seller before ordering. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills for seniors</a> covers what to look for in that case.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It does not suit runners.</strong> The listed belt lengths are short for a running
            stride, and an unverified peak motor figure is a poor basis for regular running.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It does not suit anyone who wants long support.</strong> Without a stated motor
            warranty or a named manufacturer, you have no assurance of parts or service a year or two
            in.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And it is not the obvious choice at the upper end of the price range.</strong> At
            $480 to $1,160 there are folding treadmills from makers who publish model numbers,
            warranties and manuals. Paying that much for an unidentified machine is hard to justify.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Alternatives on This Site</h2>
<p>
            The useful comparison is not with another unbranded listing but with machines whose
            figures we could cross-check.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a folding treadmill you can jog on, start with our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $500</a> and <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a> pages, and read our <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill primer</a> on how the fold mechanisms differ. If walking is the main use, a walking pad is smaller and
            cheaper: our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills under $300</a> roundup and our review of the <a href="/treaflow-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Treaflow walking pad</a> show what a carefully read budget listing looks like.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are shopping marketplaces generally, our guides to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a> and <a href="/wayfair-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying one on Wayfair</a> explain who you are actually buying from on each, and how that affects returns and
            warranty.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Streamdale Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from treating a seller’s listing as if it were a manufacturer’s specification. These four matter most.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Searching for reviews of "the" Streamdale treadmill</h3>
              <p>There is no single model, so reviews of one Streamdale listing may describe a different machine from the one you are looking at. Read reviews on the exact listing you plan to buy from, check that they describe the same features, and give more weight to specific complaints than to generic praise. Absence of reviews means you are the first test.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying at a sale price without checking returns</h3>
              <p>Streamdale's refund policy excludes on-sale items from returns. Many listings show a discounted price against a higher regular one. If the price you pay counts as a sale, you may have no change-of-mind return at all. Ask the seller in writing whether your order is returnable before you pay, and keep the reply.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Reading the horsepower figure as continuous</h3>
              <p>The listings quote 2.5 to 3.5 HP with no continuous rating, and some label the figure max or impulse. Those are peak numbers. They say little about how the motor copes with a long session or a heavier user. Compare machines on continuous ratings where available and treat a peak-only listing as incomplete.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not planning for a heavy box at the door</h3>
              <p>Ground parcel delivery typically leaves a 60 to 130 lb box at the doorstep, with no room-of-choice service or assembly. Arrange a second person, clear the route to the room, and keep the packaging until you are sure the machine works, since a return may require original packaging and you will be paying to ship it.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A Streamdale folding treadmill is a seller’s listing, not a
            model you can check.</strong> Streamdale Furniture is an online furniture reseller whose
            treadmills have no model numbers, no independent reviews we could find and no clearly
            stated motor warranty, under a return policy that excludes sale items and charges you to
            send back a remorse return.
          </p>
          <p>
            If one listing fits your needs at a low price, confirm the maker, returns and warranty in
            writing first. Otherwise, buy a folding treadmill you can identify — start with <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our under-$500 roundup</a> or the <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best folding treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

